# V7c batched-decode memory gate, 2026-10-18

Machine, model and flags as in 07-batched-decode.log (RTX 5050 Laptop, 40 W cap,
Qwen3.5-4B Q8 CUDA INT8, `--ctx 2048`, greedy). Load generator `06-load.py`,
prompts and scenarios unchanged. Gate being closed: P4 of 07 (peak process
VRAM at 3 decoders <= 128 MiB over the V7 scheduler). Not relaxed.

## Where the memory went

Per sequence at ctx 2048 (`GpuCache::bytes_held` / `checkpoint_bytes`):
83.00 MiB held = DeltaNet state about 51 MiB (24 layers of conv + recurrent)
+ one 32 MiB KV slab; plus 4.03 MiB of split-attention scratch and page table =
87.03 MiB per staged copy. V7b staged all of it for every batch member, so a
B = 3 step held 3 x 87 MiB of copies next to the 3 live caches. Each staged
copy is 52 device allocations (24 DeltaNet layers x 2, slab, page table, 2 scratch).

What a decode step really overwrites:
- KV: the pass only writes positions at or past the committed length, which no
  reader looks at until `advance` commits them. Copying the prefix (a 32 MiB slab
  and 4 MiB of scratch per sequence, and O(context) copy time) protects nothing.
- DeltaNet conv + recurrent state: overwritten in place by the pass. This is the
  only committed data a decode step destroys.

Engine-level measurements (`08-memory-probe.rs.txt`: cuMemGetInfo, 40-token
prompts, ctx 2048, device memory still held after the steps, MiB):

| Variant | B=1 | B=2 | B=3 (30 steps) | B=3 (150 steps) |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| V7b: stage everything | +32 | +96 | +192 | +288 |
| stage DeltaNet only, KV in place | +32 | +32 | +128 | +256 |
| no staging, replay on failure (kept) | +32 (B=1 uses `forward`) | 0 | 0 | 0 (-33 after) |

Staging only DeltaNet state was built and measured end to end and rejected:
server peak VRAM at 3 decoders stayed 4824-4920 MiB (V7b 4792-4856), because the
copies are 51 MiB per sequence for every step and the stream-ordered pool keeps
the high-water mark. Peak device memory is set by the copies, so the copies had
to go.

## Fix (kept)

`forward_batch` no longer stages. The pass runs on the caches in place. If it
fails after anything was written, every cache in the batch is marked `replay`:
the token ledger is untouched, the next pass for that cache rebuilds the state
from its committed tokens (the mechanism truncate/clear already use), and the
batch falls back to one pass per sequence as before. Failures are validated up
front where possible (full cache, bad token, wrong owner, token ledger
reservation), so those leave the caches untouched without any replay.

Trade-off, measured on the real model (prompt 40, 12 decode steps, then
truncate one token and feed it back through the replay path): replayed logits
differ from incrementally decoded logits by at most 5.7e-6 (max logit 12.46),
same greedy token, not bit-identical. Fixture tests assert exact equality of
logits and of the full recurrent + KV contents after a late (NaN-logit) batch
failure, for FP32 and INT8. Greedy output and the pinned HF check are unchanged.

Side effect: no per-step state copies. Engine ceiling `07-batch-probe.rs.txt`,
forward_batch vs one forward per sequence, step ms (tok/s, gain):

| B | V7b | now |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | 1.00x | 24.4 ms, 40.9 tok/s (1.00x) |
| 2 | 1.73x | 26.8 ms, 74.6 tok/s, 1.79x |
| 3 | 34.2 ms, 87.7 tok/s, 2.18x | 30.7 ms, 97.8 tok/s, 2.35x |
| 4 | 2.56x | 34.6 ms, 115.7 tok/s, 2.80x |

## Server-vs-engine overhead: one measured cause (kept)

Server decode window at B = 3 ran at about 84 events/s against an engine
ceiling of 87.7-97.8. Timing every batched step in the gate (temporary
instrumentation, removed): the leader waited 4.07 ms on average (median 4.06,
max 4.16 ms, 195 of 195 full batches) for the other decoders. Cause: a decode
step was pushed onto the queue without waking the waiting leader, so the leader
always slept to the end of COLLECT (4 ms) even when all three steps were there.
One `notify_all` after the push: wait 4.07 -> 0.48 ms mean (max 0.86 ms), pass
time unchanged (28-30 ms). decode3 aggregate 66.9 -> 69.0 events/s in the same
session with instrumentation on; 53.1 -> 54.5 in interleaved A/B without it (V7b vs now).
Remaining gap to the engine ceiling: three prompts are still prefilled one at a
time (TTFT 0.35 / 0.65 / 1.03 s), so full B = 3 decode starts after 1 s of a
5.5 s run, plus thread hand-off and sampling.

## Matched benchmark

Binaries: `v7d` = V7 sequential scheduler, `v7g` = V7b (previous batched), `v8` =
this change. The machine slowed about 18% after the first session (V7 solo decode
36.0 -> 29.5 events/s, same binary), so only sessions in the same regime are
compared; the V7b/this-change pairs below were interleaved (A B A B A B).
Medians; TTFT over all clients; completion = per-request total.

| Scenario | V7 | V7b | now |
|---|---|---|---|
| decode1 events/s | 29.5 | 29.7 | 29.3 |
| decode2 events/s (per stream) | 29.1 (15.4) | 45.1 (24.7) | 44.5 (24.8) |
| decode3 events/s (per stream) | 29.0 (10.2) | 53.1 (19.7) | **54.5 (20.4)** |
| decode3 TTFT p50 / p95 | 0.72 / 1.15 | 0.71 / 1.16 | 0.73 / 1.17 |
| decode3 completion mean / max | 13.18 / 13.33 s | 7.17 / 7.38 | 6.95 / 7.18 |
| decode3 completion spread in a run | 0.3% | 1.0% | 1.7% |
| three_interactive events/s | 17.3 | 20.5 | 20.6 |
| three_interactive TTFT p50 / p95 | 0.65 / 1.07 | 0.67 / 1.07 | 0.67 / 1.09 |
| three_interactive completion mean / max | 2.05 / 2.47 s | 1.61 / 2.00 | 1.60 / 2.04 |
| long_plus_two interactive TTFT p95; long job | 0.85 s; 14.3 s | 0.70 s; 13.7 | 0.82 s; 14.1 |
| solo_short TTFT / total (interleaved, n=15) | 0.323 / 0.571 s | 0.338 / 0.548 | 0.330 / 0.532 |
| solo_long TTFT / total (interleaved, n=9) | 10.59 / 12.69 s | 10.45 / 12.52 | 10.50 / 12.58 |

decode3 gain over V7 in the same regime: 54.5 / 29.0 = 1.88x (V7b 1.83x).
An early session pair showed solo_long 4% slower and three_interactive 9% slower
than V7b; both vanished in interleaved runs (drift, not the change).

Process VRAM peak (nvidia-smi, 2 Hz), MiB, min-max over every run of every
session of that binary, and RSS:

| Scenario | V7 | V7b | now |
|---|---|---|---|
| decode3 | 4632-4792 | 4792-4856 | **4632-4696** |
| three_interactive | 4600-4728 | 4632-4792 | 4568-4664 |
| long_plus_two | 4696-4792 | 4728-4792 | 4664-4696 |
| decode3 RSS | 278-279 | 300-311 | 296-315 |

Same-session decode3 pairs (interleaved): V7 4696-4760, V7b 4792-4856, now
4632-4696. Growth over V7 at 3 decoders: <= 0 MiB by either comparison (gate
128 MiB); over V7b it is -96 to -160 MiB. RSS grows 18-36 MiB (response buffers
of the extra concurrent decoders; not part of the gate, which allows 128 MiB).
Device free memory stayed above 2.3 GB.

## Correctness

- Greedy text of every client in decode1/2/3, three_interactive and long_plus_two
  equals the V7 scheduler's in every run (3-6 runs per scenario).
- Batch vs sequential logits bit-identical, B = 2, 3 (4 on the fixture), real
  model, 12 steps (`qwen35_batch.rs`); a full cache in a batch is rejected and
  leaves both caches unchanged.
- Late batch failure (NaN norm after all state was written): ledgers intact,
  replay flag set, next passes match an untouched twin, state contents equal
  (`gpu_tests.rs`, FP32 and INT8 fixtures).
- behave.py on the new binary: concurrent sessions isolated, same ID in use -> 404,
  disconnect mid-prefill frees the GPU (next request 2.59 s), 3 x 200 + 2 x 408 +
  extras 503. A client dropping 2 s into a three-way batched decode leaves the
  two survivors with V7-identical text, and the same prompt afterwards also
  matches. No errors in the server log.
- Session checkpoint pairs (ctx 512, slower regime): code pair 3.80 s vs 5.06 s
  reuse off, turn-2 TTFT 0.488 s, 170 reused; chat pair 3.94 s vs 5.33 s, 0.511 s,
  186 reused. Reuse counts equal 07; texts equal.
- fmt, check, Clippy -D warnings, line-limit, unsafe clean. Workspace tests with
  MARIN_REQUIRE_CUDA=1 and with MARIN_MATMUL_SCALAR=1: 646 passed, 0 failed,
  5 ignored each. Pinned HF verify unchanged: max logit 1.31e-5, max layer
  1.18e-5, normalized max 3.879e-6, relative RMS 3.288e-6.

## Gate evaluation

| Gate | Result |
|---|---|
| P4 peak process VRAM growth <= 128 MiB at B = 3 | **PASS: <= 0 MiB** (4632-4696 vs V7 4632-4792; same session 4696-4760) |
| Throughput keeps meaningful B = 3 gain (>= 1.5x V7) | PASS: 1.88x (V7b 1.83x) |
| TTFT / completion / solo not worse than V7b by > 5% | PASS: within noise in interleaved runs |
| Exact greedy and HF parity, atomicity, cancellation, isolation, checkpoints | PASS (see above); post-failure replay is within 5.7e-6, not bit-exact |
| Server-vs-engine overhead: one measured cause | Fixed: 4.07 ms collection wait -> 0.48 ms, +3% aggregate |

## Admission

`max_active` no longer reserves one staged copy per batch member (V7b: free
memory / state bytes minus `batch_limit + 1`); a batched decode step stages
nothing, so it reserves the idle session and the one prefill copy (minus 2). Still
3 on this machine at ctx 2048; tighter configurations admit more.


